question:
Consider this DLL node deletion logic:

```c
void deleteNode(Node* del) {
    if (del->prev != NULL)
        del->prev->next = del->next;
    if (del->next != NULL)
        del->next->prev = del->prev;
    free(del);
}
```

Which structural scenario is NOT handled by this code, potentially leaving the list in a broken state?

When implementing a Queue using a Linked List, a common mistake is to `free()` the dequeued node *before* reading its data. Consider:

```c
int dequeue(Queue* q) {
    Node* temp = q->front;
    free(temp);               // freed here
    q->front = temp->next;    // accessing freed memory
    return temp->data;        // accessing freed memory
}
```

What is the technical name for this class of bug?
